\section{Setting up the diagnostic quizzes, literately}
\label{app:quiz}

This appendix is a literate program \autocite{LiterateProgramming}: the
prose and the code are one and the same source, woven into the text you
are reading and tangled into the files the computer runs.
It implements the pre/post-test of \cref{sec:method-instruments}: two
quizzes that collect two kinds of data before and after the LaTeX module.
The closed items measure whether the students discern the candidate
critical aspects of \cref{sec:compilation,sec:errors,sec:markup}; the open
items collect the students' own accounts of what LaTeX is, of a mistake
seen in the PDF, of a PDF that has not changed, of an error message, of
two headings that look alike and of two question marks in the output,
from which the phenomenographic analysis of \cref{sec:method-instruments}
forms its categories of description.
Three files are tangled from this section:
[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]] and [[quiz-knowledge-end.json]], the
descriptions of the two quizzes that the [[canvaslms]] tool pushes to
Canvas, and [[analyze_quiz.py]], which fetches the results from Canvas,
summarises the closed items and writes the open accounts to a coding
sheet.

\subsection{The two quizzes for Canvas}

The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates the quizzes from their JSON descriptions:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms modules create -c datintro26 -p 6 "LaTeX pre-test"
canvaslms modules create -c datintro26 -p 8 "LaTeX post-test"
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz-knowledge-start.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz-knowledge-end.json
canvaslms modules edit -c datintro26 \
  --prerequisite "^Collaboration$" --prerequisite "^LaTeX pre-test$" \
  "^Report writing$"
canvaslms modules edit -c datintro26 \
  --prerequisite "^Report writing$" "^LaTeX post-test$"
\end{minted}
Each quiz is the only item of a module of its own, created first: the
pre-test module sits just before the module that holds the LaTeX
material (\enquote{Report writing} in the course) and the post-test
module just after it.
The reason is that Canvas locks a module behind the completion of the
modules it names as prerequisites, and only whole modules can be
prerequisites.
With the pre-test in its own module, the report-writing module can
require it, so that no student reads the material before writing the
pre-test; with the post-test in its own module, it can require the
report-writing module, so that no student writes the post-test before
the material.
The last two commands set up exactly that chain; since editing a
module's prerequisites replaces the whole list, the first of them
restates the prerequisite the report-writing module already had.
The placement is part of the quiz description, under the key
[[modules]], which [[canvaslms]] reads and strips before it talks to
Canvas: a module name, anchored so that the pre-test module cannot match
the post-test module, the position within it, and the completion
requirement---submitting the quiz---without which a module counts as
complete the moment it is opened and the prerequisite would lock
nothing.
The modules and the quizzes are created unpublished; the teacher
releases them when the module opens.
A later change to this source is applied to the quizzes already in
Canvas with [[canvaslms --no-cache quizzes edit -c datintro26 -f <file>]],
which updates the settings and, with [[--replace-items]], replaces every
item from the file; the module placement is untouched, and the tool stops
to ask if any student has already submitted.
([[--no-cache]] because the tool's cache of the course's quizzes does not
yet know the quizzes the tool itself created; without it the quiz is
\enquote{not found}.)
The two quizzes share their open items and, with one exception explained
with the item (\cref{app:knowledge-items}), their knowledge items; they
differ in what opens them.
The start-of-module quiz measures what the students bring to the module;
the end-of-module quiz repeats the same items to measure the gain, and
adds one question of its own---whether the student prepared---because
preparation would confound that gain: a student who reviews the material
the night before measures preparation, not what the module left behind.
The quiz titles name LaTeX explicitly, since the course runs other
diagnostic quizzes (on the terminal and on Git) that must stay
distinguishable for students and analysis alike.
The instructions warn that some questions ask for an account in the
student's own words, so that a student who expects only multiple choice
does not skip them as odd.

<<[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "LaTeX quiz: what you know already",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz checks what you already know about writing documents with LaTeX, so that we can adapt the course and, together with the quiz at the end of the module, measure how well the material works. The questions show short pieces of LaTeX, what comes out of it, and messages it prints; if you have not used LaTeX before, answer as well as you can&mdash;guessing is fine, and a low score is expected and completely okay. Six questions ask you to write in your own words; there are no wrong answers there, and they carry no points. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers&mdash;the course will teach you those. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 10,
    <<start-quiz module>>
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<start result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<start-quiz consent item>>,
    <<open items>>,
    <<knowledge items>>
  ]
}
@

The end-of-module quiz carries the same open and knowledge items, so the
two administrations measure the same discernment and collect comparable
accounts; the opening item and the instructions differ, and the end quiz
adds the closed twin of the open item on a mistake seen in the PDF,
which the start quiz asks only in the open form
(\cref{app:knowledge-items}).

<<[[quiz-knowledge-end.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "LaTeX quiz: what you know now",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz repeats the LaTeX questions from the start-of-module quiz, so that we can see what you have learned during the module. Six questions ask you to write in your own words; there are no wrong answers there, and they carry no points. Afterwards you will see your score and, for each question, the correct answer. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without preparation, help or tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 11,
    <<end-quiz module>>
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<end result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<end-quiz preparation item>>,
    <<open items>>,
    <<item: where the change goes>>,
    <<knowledge items>>
  ]
}
@

The module keys name the modules created above; the quiz is the first
and only item of its module, and the module is complete once the quiz
is submitted.

<<start-quiz module>>=
"modules": [{"module": "^LaTeX pre-test$", "position": 1,
             "completion_requirement": "must_submit"}],
<<end-quiz module>>=
"modules": [{"module": "^LaTeX post-test$", "position": 1,
             "completion_requirement": "must_submit"}],
@

Both quizzes are diagnostics: there is no time limit, a single attempt
suffices, and answers are never shuffled (several items show source
whose order is meaningful, and the preparation item's choices are
ordered).
The items are shown one at a time, without going back, for a reason
that \cref{app:open-items} spells out: the open accounts must be
written before the student has seen the alternatives of the closed
items, and on a single page the order of the items would govern only the
order of reading.
The cost of forbidding revisits is small in a zero-stakes diagnostic; it
is the price of clean accounts.
The scheduling keys are a flat list at the level of the quiz settings,
and so are the interaction keys inside [[quiz_settings]]; the attempt
and result-view settings, however, are objects of their own inside
[[quiz_settings]], because that is how the platform's API groups them
and it silently drops the same keys written flat.
So each chunk opens and closes exactly the braces it owns: the
[[quiz_settings]] object opens and closes in the quiz descriptions
above, and the result-view chunks below open and close theirs.

<<scheduling settings>>=
"time_limit": null,
"due_at": null,
"unlock_at": null,
"lock_at": null,
<<interaction settings>>=
"shuffle_answers": false,
"shuffle_questions": false,
"has_time_limit": false,
"one_at_a_time_type": "question",
"allow_backtracking": false,
"multiple_attempts": {"multiple_attempts_enabled": false},
@

The two quizzes differ in what they show the student afterwards.
The start quiz shows the points awarded---how well the student did---but
neither per-item correctness nor the correct answers: the same questions
must measure the gain at the end of the module, so they have to stay
fresh.
The end quiz has nothing left to protect, and the students have earned
the answers: it shows, item by item, whether the answer was right and
which alternative was, so that the diagnostic also teaches---the
distractor a student chose is the misconception they can now read against
the correct account.
It also shows, under each item, a short text of ours: for a closed item
why the keyed alternative is right and what each distractor assumes, and
for an open item the ways of seeing we expect, from the first impression
to the one the module aims at (\cref{app:open-items-feedback}).
That text travels with the item, and the items are shared by the two
quizzes, so the start quiz carries it too; the last key of each object is
the one switch that hides it there and shows it at the end, and the
deployment reads the settings back to confirm the switch took.
The platform applies none of this unless the view is declared
restricted, hence the first key of each object.

<<start result view>>=
"result_view_settings": {
  "result_view_restricted": true,
  "display_points_awarded": true,
  "display_points_possible": true,
  "display_items": true,
  "display_item_response": true,
  "display_item_response_correctness": false,
  "display_item_correct_answer": false,
  "display_item_feedback": false
}
<<end result view>>=
"result_view_settings": {
  "result_view_restricted": true,
  "display_points_awarded": true,
  "display_points_possible": true,
  "display_items": true,
  "display_item_response": true,
  "display_item_response_correctness": true,
  "display_item_correct_answer": true,
  "display_item_feedback": true
}
@

\subsection{The consent and preparation items}

The start-of-module quiz opens with the consent item---an arrangement
kept from the course's earlier diagnostic tests, which these quizzes
replace:
participation in the study is opt-in, so we ask before collecting
anything.
The item titles double as the keys under which Canvas reports the
answers, so the analysis program (\cref{app:quiz-analysis}) refers to
the items by these titles.

