\subsection{The open accounts}
\label{app:open-items}

The open items are the phenomenographic data: the students' own accounts
of what LaTeX is, of what to do about a mistake seen in the PDF, of a PDF
that does not change when the file does, of what an error message says,
of two headings that look alike, and of two question marks where a
section number should be.
Every student writes about the same stimulus, so the accounts are
comparable across students and between the start and the end of the
module; they are read for qualitatively different ways of experiencing
the phenomenon, sorted into categories of description and ordered into
an outcome space (\cref{sec:method-instruments}).
The stems present a concrete situation and ask for the student's own
account; none of them names an aspect, for the same reason as the closed
items below \autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}: a stem that says \enquote{recompile}
or \enquote{the source} has already done the discerning.
The closing invitation---\enquote{if you do not know the commands,
describe in words}---matters at the start of the module, where many
students have never seen a LaTeX file: an account in words of what they
would want to happen is exactly the data we are after, and without the
invitation those students would leave the box empty.

The open items come \emph{before} the closed items.
The closed items' distractors spell out the candidate conceptions in
full---\enquote{correct the letters in the PDF; the LaTeX file updates
itself to match}, \enquote{the change appears in the PDF by itself after
a few seconds}---so a student who has just read a dozen such items has
been handed the outcome space, and an account written afterwards echoes
the alternatives rather than the student's own way of seeing.
The other direction is harmless: an open stem shows only a situation,
and whatever small effect writing about it has on the closed answers is
the same in both administrations, so the pre/post gain is unaffected.
This is why the quizzes show one item at a time without backtracking
(\cref{app:quiz}).

<<open items>>=
<<item: what LaTeX is>>,
<<item: a mistake in the PDF>>,
<<item: the PDF has not changed>>,
<<item: what this message says>>,
<<item: two headings that look alike>>,
<<item: two question marks>>
@

All open items carry no points and no correct answer; Canvas's essay
interaction needs only the flag that turns off the rich-text editor (a
plain box invites plain prose) and the word limit.
The items occupy positions 3--8, leaving position 2 free for a further
opening item, just as the knowledge items leave room below 11.

The first item asks for the student's conception of LaTeX itself and of
what happens between the text they write and the document they see.
It is the LaTeX counterpart of the question \enquote{what does the word
Unix mean to you?} with which the companion study opens its accounts of
the terminal \autocite{VTTerminal}, rephrased for a tool the student may
never have used.
The stem says \enquote{the text you wrote} and \enquote{the document you
look at}, not \enquote{source} and \enquote{output}: that there are two
artifacts and that one is derived from the other is the first aspect of
\cref{sec:compilation}, and a stem that named them as such would be doing
the discerning \autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.

