\subsection{The knowledge items}
\label{app:knowledge-items}

The knowledge items are written for this study and published here in
full.
They walk through the three educational objectives of the module in turn:
the compile model (\cref{sec:compilation}), error handling
(\cref{sec:errors}), and semantic markup (\cref{sec:markup}); each item
tests one candidate critical aspect, and whether the student discerns it
is exactly what the item measures.
Following \citeauthor{NCOL}'s discussion of assessment, the stems do not
point out the relevant aspect---discerning it is what we measure
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}---so each stem shows only LaTeX, what comes out of
it, or a message it prints, and leaves what matters for the student to
see.
Each distractor expresses the behaviour of a learner who has not
discerned the aspect, and throughout the distractors are the
WYSIWYG-word-processor expectations the module has to displace: that the
document \emph{is} what the screen shows.
The correct alternative is kept about as long as the distractors, since
the longest alternative is a known cue for test-wise students.
All items weigh one point: we measure discernment by counting correct
answers, and \cref{app:quiz-analysis} reports them item by item, since
each item stands for one aspect.
Each item is followed by the text the end quiz shows under it, with the
correct alternative: a short account of why that alternative is right and
what each distractor assumes---the distractor a student chose is the
misconception they can now read against it.
The text may name the aspect, since it is shown only after the answer and
only at the end; it paraphrases the rationale given before the item, and
the two must be kept in step.

The items are numbered from position~11 so that their placement is
independent of the quiz they appear in: Canvas orders by position, gaps
are fine, and the numbering leaves room for the quiz-specific opening
item (consent or preparation) and the open accounts before them.
The list below is the ten items both quizzes share; the eleventh,
\emph{where the change goes}, is in the end quiz only and is listed
there.

