\subsubsection{The compile model}

Two artifacts, one direction (\cref{sec:compilation}): edits happen in the
LaTeX file, and the PDF is derived from it, never the other way round.
The distractors edit the PDF, or expect editing the PDF to change the
file---the WYSIWYG assumption that the document one sees is the document
one edits.
This item is the one the start quiz leaves out: there the same mistake
is the open item \emph{a mistake in the PDF} (\cref{app:open-items}),
because the alternatives spell out the model---that the correction goes
into the file and the PDF is made anew---to students who may be meeting
LaTeX for the first time, and the start quiz promises not to reveal what
the course will teach.
The end quiz carries both forms, so for this aspect the movement is read
from the account at the start to the item at the end, and the paired
per-item gain of \cref{app:quiz-analysis} is computed over the other
items only.
The item keeps position 11 in the end quiz; the start quiz simply has a
gap there.

