Structure nests (\cref{sec:compilation}): an environment opened with
[[\begin]] is closed by its [[\end]], a brace by its partner, and what
lies between belongs to it---a list begun inside a list is inside it.
This is the one aspect of the compile model read off a documented
difficulty: novices learning a markup language grapple with
\enquote{hierarchies and paths, nesting} as much as with the notation
\autocite{Park2011WebDev}, and reading hierarchies in code---LaTeX among
the languages---has an instrument of its own \autocite{Park2016Hierarchies}.
The stem shows a nested list and asks what comes out; it does not say
\enquote{nested} or \enquote{inside}, which is the aspect
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.
The distractors flatten the structure, take the inner list for a second
list after the first, or take the nesting for an error.

