\subsection{Analysing the results}
\label{app:quiz-analysis}

Canvas compiles the responses into a student-analysis report: a CSV file
with one row per student and one column per item, keyed by the item
titles above.
The analysis program fetches that report itself---we know the courses and
the quiz titles, so nothing needs to be clicked or downloaded by
hand---using the same [[canvaslms]] machinery as the command-line tool.
For the closed items it tabulates the opening items, reports each
knowledge item's facility and distractor counts, summarises the scores
and, given both reports, the paired gains: the movement per student and
per aspect that \cref{sec:method-instruments} asks for.
For the open items it writes the accounts to a coding sheet: one row per
student, quiz and item, with empty columns for the codes, so that every
collected account is read and coded.

The coding is done by a human, but a large language model prepares it:
with [[--llm]] the program asks a model (via the [[llm]] package) to
suggest, for each account, a category in the student's own terms and
what the student discerns and does not, and the coder verifies, merges
or overrides these in the sheet.
The suggestions are kept in separate columns from the verified codes, so
the sheet always shows which is which; and they are deliberately not
drawn from a fixed list, since the categories are what the analysis is
to find.

<<[[analyze_quiz.py]]>>=
"""Fetch and summarise the LaTeX-quiz results, and prepare the coding
sheet.

The quizzes are defined in the two quiz-knowledge-*.json files, tangled
from the same literate source (the appendix of the paper).  The results
come straight from Canvas via canvaslms; the item titles are the column
keys, and the answer key is read from the quiz description.

Requires canvaslms and its configured credentials; the optional LLM
pre-coding requires the llm package with a default model.
"""

import argparse
import collections
import csv
import html
import json
import os
import re
import statistics
import sys

<<the courses and the item titles are the keys>>
<<the answer key from the quiz description>>
<<fetch the results from Canvas>>
<<attach the login IDs>>
<<the consent filter>>
<<tabulate the opening items>>
<<per-item facility and distractor frequencies>>
<<paired pre/post gain>>
<<pre-code the open accounts with an LLM>>
<<write the coding sheet>>
<<analysis main>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@

The course names and the item titles are the join keys between the
tangled files: if a title changes in a quiz, this block is the only
other place to change---and they live in the same source, side by side.
The module runs in two courses: datintro26, where the literate program
created the quizzes, and the CS programme's course alongside it, into
which the teachers copy the module---quizzes included---by hand.
The analysis therefore fetches the report from every course in the list
that has the quiz, pools the rows, and tags each row with its course, so
the cohorts stay separable; a course or quiz not found is noted and
skipped, since the copy is made by hand and may lag this source.
A name cannot identify a student: two students may share one even
within a course, let alone across the pooled ones.
The identifier is the student's \emph{login ID}---an email address,
unique across the institution and beyond, and the identifier the
surrounding tooling already keys results by---which the report does not
carry, so [[fetch_results]] attaches it to every row from the course's
own roster (\cref{app:quiz-analysis}).
Every join below goes through [[student_of]], which uses the login ID
and falls back to the course--name pair only for rows read from a saved
report, which lack the attached column.
% TODO(#6): confirm the CS course's exact Canvas course code when the
% 2026 instance exists (the 2025 instance's code was prginda25, not
% progd25).
The coding columns are the open scheme: a category of description, what
the account shows the student discerning and not discerning, and notes;
the suggested columns mirror them and add the model's rationale.
Canvas prefixes the report's columns with item identifiers, so we match
columns by containment rather than equality---which is why no item title
may be a substring of another's.

<<the courses and the item titles are the keys>>=
COURSES = ["datintro26", "progd26"]
COURSE_COLUMN = "course"
LOGIN_COLUMN = "login"
QUIZ_TITLES = {
    "start": "LaTeX quiz: what you know already",
    "end": "LaTeX quiz: what you know now",
}
QUIZ_FILE = "quiz-knowledge-end.json"
STUDENT = "name"
CONSENT = "Consent"
CONTROL_ITEMS = [CONSENT, "Preparation"]
OPEN_ITEMS = ["What LaTeX is", "A mistake in the PDF",
              "The PDF has not changed", "What this message says",
              "Two headings that look alike", "Two question marks"]
CODED_KEYS = ["category", "discerned", "undiscerned"]
CODING_COLUMNS = CODED_KEYS + ["notes"]
SUGGESTED_COLUMNS = [f"suggested_{key}" for key in CODED_KEYS + ["rationale"]]


def column_for(title, fieldnames):
    """Return the report column whose name contains the item title."""
    return next((name for name in fieldnames if title in name), None)


def student_of(row):
    """Return the identifier of the student behind a row.

    The login ID attached by fetch_results when available; rows read
    from a saved report lack it and fall back to the course--name pair.
    """
    return row.get(LOGIN_COLUMN) or (row.get(COURSE_COLUMN, ""), row[STUDENT])
@

\subsubsection{The answer key}

The report records each closed answer as the text of the chosen
alternative, not its number, so the program needs the alternatives and
the key.
Rather than duplicating them by hand---a second copy that would drift
from the first---it reads them from the tangled quiz description: the
end quiz, since it carries every item, whereas the start quiz leaves one
knowledge item out (\cref{app:knowledge-items}).
An item a quiz does not carry has no column in that quiz's report, so
the tables below ask first whether the item was asked at all, and skip
it rather than count every student as wrong.
The item bodies are HTML, so the text is stripped of tags and unescaped
before it is compared with the report; both sides are reduced to single
spaces, since Canvas is free to rewrap.
The graded choice items are the knowledge items (the preparation item is
a choice item too, but carries no points); the essay items are the open
items, whose stems the pre-coding prompt quotes.
The feedback an item carries is for the student and is not read here: in
particular the provisional outcome spaces of \cref{app:open-items-feedback}
never reach the coding sheet or the pre-coding prompt.

<<the answer key from the quiz description>>=
TAGS = re.compile(r"<[^>]+>")


def plain(body):
    """Return the text of an HTML item body, whitespace normalised."""
    return " ".join(html.unescape(TAGS.sub(" ", body)).split())


def load_quiz(path):
    """Return the knowledge items and the open stems of a quiz file.

    The knowledge items map title -> (alternatives, correct position);
    the open stems map title -> stem text.
    """
    with open(path) as infile:
        quiz = json.load(infile)
    knowledge, stems = {}, {}
    for item in quiz["items"]:
        entry = item["entry"]
        kind = entry["interaction_type_slug"]
        if kind == "choice" and item["points_possible"]:
            choices = sorted(entry["interaction_data"]["choices"],
                             key=lambda choice: choice["position"])
            knowledge[entry["title"]] = (
                [plain(choice["item_body"]) for choice in choices],
                entry["scoring_data"]["value"],
            )
        elif kind == "essay":
            stems[entry["title"]] = plain(entry["item_body"])
    return knowledge, stems


def answer_of(row, title):
    """Return the student's answer to the titled item, normalised."""
    column = column_for(title, row.keys())
    return plain(row.get(column, "")) if column else ""


def is_correct(row, title, knowledge):
    """Whether the row's answer to the titled item is the keyed one."""
    choices, correct = knowledge[title]
    return answer_of(row, title) == choices[correct - 1]


def asked(rows, title):
    """Whether the report's columns include the titled item at all."""
    return bool(rows) and column_for(title, rows[0].keys()) is not None
@

\subsubsection{Fetching the report from Canvas}

Just as we create the quizzes with [[canvaslms]], we extract the results
with it: the same package provides the credentials, the course lookup and
the report machinery, so the program reuses them instead of
reimplementing the Canvas API calls.
The report is generated on demand by Canvas, so we request it, wait for
it, and download the CSV it points to---once per course that has the
quiz, appending the course name to each row under [[COURSE_COLUMN]].

<<fetch the results from Canvas>>=
def fetch_results(which):
    """Download the student-analysis rows for the start or end quiz.

    Fetches from every course in COURSES that has the quiz, pools the
    rows and tags each with its course; missing courses or quizzes are
    noted and skipped.
    """
    import appdirs
    import requests
    import canvaslms.cli
    import canvaslms.cli.login
    from canvasapi import Canvas
    from canvaslms.cli.quizzes import (
        create_new_quiz_report,
        filter_quizzes,
        is_new_quiz,
        poll_progress,
    )

    config_file = os.path.join(
        appdirs.user_config_dir("canvaslms"), "config.json"
    )
    config = canvaslms.cli.read_configuration(config_file)
    hostname, token = canvaslms.cli.login.load_credentials(config)
    if not hostname.startswith("http"):
        hostname = f"https://{hostname}"
    canvas = Canvas(hostname, token)

    rows = []
    for name in COURSES:
        course = next(
            (c for c in canvas.get_courses()
             if name in (c.name or "") or name in (c.course_code or "")),
            None,
        )
        if course is None:
            print(f"note: no course matching {name}, skipping")
            continue
        quiz = next(filter_quizzes([course], QUIZ_TITLES[which]), None)
        if quiz is None:
            print(f"note: {name} has no quiz {QUIZ_TITLES[which]!r}, skipping")
            continue
        if not is_new_quiz(quiz):
            sys.exit(f"quiz {quiz.title!r} in {name} is not a New Quiz")

        progress = poll_progress(
            create_new_quiz_report(course, quiz.id, canvas._Canvas__requester)
        )
        url = progress.results.get("url") if progress else None
        if not url:
            sys.exit(f"Canvas did not produce the report for {name}")
        response = requests.get(url)
        response.raise_for_status()
        course_rows = list(csv.DictReader(response.text.splitlines()))
        for row in course_rows:
            row[COURSE_COLUMN] = name
        attach_logins(course_rows, course, name)
        rows.extend(course_rows)
    return rows
@

The report identifies its rows by name and, in its [[id]] and [[sis_id]]
columns, by the platform's and the institution's identifiers; the login
ID lives in the course roster.
So each row is looked up in the roster: by the platform ID, else by the
institutional ID, else---for a report that carries neither---by name,
which is accepted only when exactly one enrolled student bears it; an
ambiguous name is reported rather than silently mis-joined, and such
rows keep the course--name fallback of [[student_of]].

<<attach the login IDs>>=
def attach_logins(rows, course, name):
    """Attach each row's student login ID from the course roster."""
    by_id, by_sis, by_name = {}, {}, {}
    try:
        students = list(course.get_users(enrollment_type=["student"]))
    except Exception as error:
        print(f"warning: cannot read the roster of {name}: {error}")
        return
    for user in students:
        login = getattr(user, "login_id", None)
        if not login:
            continue
        by_id[str(user.id)] = login
        sis = getattr(user, "sis_user_id", None)
        if sis:
            by_sis[str(sis)] = login
        by_name.setdefault(user.name, []).append(login)
    if not rows:
        return
    id_column = next((c for c in rows[0] if c.strip().lower() == "id"), None)
    sis_column = next((c for c in rows[0]
                       if c.strip().lower() == "sis_id"), None)
    for row in rows:
        login = None
        if id_column and row.get(id_column):
            login = by_id.get(str(row[id_column]).strip())
        if not login and sis_column and row.get(sis_column):
            login = by_sis.get(str(row[sis_column]).strip())
        if not login:
            candidates = by_name.get(row[STUDENT], [])
            if len(candidates) == 1:
                login = candidates[0]
            elif len(candidates) > 1:
                print(f"warning: several students in {name} are named"
                      f" {row[STUDENT]!r} and the report has no usable ID"
                      f" column; falling back to course and name")
        if login:
            row[LOGIN_COLUMN] = login
@

\subsubsection{Consent}

Consent is asked once, in the start quiz, and covers both; the end quiz
has no consent item of its own, so its rows are filtered by the names
that consented at the start (\cref{sec:method-instruments}).
Analysed on its own, the end quiz therefore cannot be filtered, and the
program says so rather than silently keeping everyone.

<<the consent filter>>=
def consenting(rows):
    """Return the students who consented, or None if unasked.

    Students are login IDs (or the course--name fallback), so namesakes
    stay distinct.
    """
    column = column_for(CONSENT, rows[0].keys())
    if column is None:
        return None
    return {student_of(row) for row in rows
            if row[column].strip().lower() in ("yes", "true")}


def filter_consent(rows, students):
    """Keep the rows of the given students; None keeps every row."""
    if students is None:
        print("note: no consent item in these rows, so no filtering")
        return rows
    return [row for row in rows if student_of(row) in students]
@

\subsubsection{Summaries}

Tabulating an opening item is counting its answer frequencies; we print
one table per opening item present in the report (the consent item only
exists in the start quiz, the preparation item only in the end quiz).
Since Canvas grades the knowledge items, each report carries a score
column, which we summarise: the score distribution at the start measures
what the students bring, and at the end, the gain.

<<tabulate the opening items>>=
def tabulate(rows, item):
    """Print the answer frequencies for one opening item."""
    column = column_for(item, rows[0].keys())
    if column is None:
        return
    counts = collections.Counter(row[column] for row in rows)
    print(f"\n{item}")
    for answer, count in counts.most_common():
        print(f"  {answer or '(no answer)'}: {count}")


def summarize_scores(rows):
    """Print the score distribution, if the report has scores."""
    column = column_for("score", rows[0].keys())
    if column is None:
        return
    scores = [float(row[column]) for row in rows if row[column]]
    if scores:
        print(f"\nscores: n={len(scores)}"
              f", mean={statistics.mean(scores):.1f}"
              f", median={statistics.median(scores):.1f}"
              f", max={max(scores):.0f}")
@

The total score says how much the students discern; which aspects they
discern, and which misconceptions they hold instead, is in the
alternatives.
So the knowledge items are reported one by one: the facility, that is
the share of answering students who chose the keyed alternative, and the
count for every alternative, the key marked with a star.
A distractor chosen by a third of the students at the start and by
nobody at the end is the clearest evidence the instrument can give that
the module made the aspect discernible; a distractor that survives the
module is where the teaching must change.

<<per-item facility and distractor frequencies>>=
def item_table(rows, knowledge):
    """Print, per knowledge item, the facility and each alternative's count."""
    for title, (choices, correct) in knowledge.items():
        if not asked(rows, title):
            continue
        answers = [answer_of(row, title) for row in rows]
        answered = [answer for answer in answers if answer]
        counts = collections.Counter(answered)
        facility = (counts[choices[correct - 1]] / len(answered)
                    if answered else float("nan"))
        print(f"\n{title}: n={len(answered)}, facility={facility:.2f}")
        for position, choice in enumerate(choices, start=1):
            mark = "*" if position == correct else " "
            print(f"  {mark} {counts[choice]:3d}  {choice}")
@

\subsubsection{Paired gains}

The gain is a within-student quantity: the same student, the same item,
before and after.
The two reports are joined on the student---the login ID, so that
namesakes never pair, not even within one course---and for each item the
program counts how many of the paired students were right at the start,
how many at the end, and---the two numbers that matter---how many moved
from wrong to right and from right to wrong; they are the discordant
cells of McNemar's test, should the numbers warrant one, and they are
reported as counts so that the reader can judge them without one.
The score gain is reported as a mean over the same pairs.

<<paired pre/post gain>>=
def paired_gain(start, end, knowledge):
    """Print, per item, how the students who took both quizzes moved."""
    at_end = {student_of(row): row for row in end}
    pairs = [(row, at_end[student_of(row)])
             for row in start if student_of(row) in at_end]
    print(f"\n{len(pairs)} students took both quizzes")
    print(f"{'item':42s} {'start':>5s} {'end':>5s} {'+':>4s} {'-':>4s}")
    for title in knowledge:
        if not (asked(start, title) and asked(end, title)):
            continue
        before = [is_correct(row, title, knowledge) for row, _ in pairs]
        after = [is_correct(row, title, knowledge) for _, row in pairs]
        gained = sum(1 for b, a in zip(before, after) if a and not b)
        lost = sum(1 for b, a in zip(before, after) if b and not a)
        print(f"{title:42s} {sum(before):5d} {sum(after):5d}"
              f" {gained:4d} {lost:4d}")
    <<the mean score gain over the pairs>>
@

The score columns may be missing (a report without grades) or empty for
a student who never submitted, so only pairs with both scores count.

<<the mean score gain over the pairs>>=
gains = []
for first, second in pairs:
    before = column_for("score", first.keys())
    after = column_for("score", second.keys())
    if before and after and first[before] and second[after]:
        gains.append(float(second[after]) - float(first[before]))
if gains:
    print(f"mean score gain={statistics.mean(gains):.2f} over {len(gains)}")
@

\subsubsection{The coding sheet}

The accounts are coded one at a time, each against the stem it answers,
so the model is given one account and one stem per call.
The prompt states the purpose---to find the qualitatively different ways
the students experience the phenomenon---and asks for a category in the
student's own terms and for what the account shows the student
discerning and not discerning; it does not offer a list to choose from,
because a list would turn the finding into confirming.
The model must answer in JSON so that the suggestions land in columns;
an unparseable answer leaves the suggestion empty rather than stopping
the run.
The suggestions are exactly that: \cref{sec:method-instruments} requires
a human coder, who forms the categories by comparing the accounts with
each other, and the verified codes go in the bare columns.

<<pre-code the open accounts with an LLM>>=
CODING_PROMPT = """\
You are pre-coding one written answer from a diagnostic quiz on
writing documents with LaTeX, for a phenomenographic analysis.  The
aim is to find the qualitatively different ways the students
experience the phenomenon, in their own terms; do not map the answer
onto a fixed list of codes.
Answer in JSON with keys "category" (one short phrase for what the
student seems to take the phenomenon to be), "discerned" (short
phrases: the distinctions the student makes), "undiscerned" (short
phrases: what the student treats as the same, or leaves out, that
matters for the task), and "rationale" (one sentence pointing at the
words in the answer).

Question: {question}
Answer: {answer}
"""


def llm_precode(records):
    """Ask the default llm model to suggest codes for each account."""
    import llm

    model = llm.get_model()
    suggestions = []
    for record in records:
        prompt = CODING_PROMPT.format(question=record["stem"],
                                      answer=record["answer"])
        try:
            suggestion = json.loads(model.prompt(prompt).text())
        except Exception:
            suggestion = {}
        suggestions.append(
            {f"suggested_{key}": suggestion.get(key, "")
             for key in CODED_KEYS + ["rationale"]}
        )
    return suggestions
@

The sheet is in long format---one row per student, quiz and item, with
the course the student belongs to and the login ID that identifies
them---for two reasons.
The categories are formed per phenomenon, so the coder reads all the
accounts of one item together, which a sort on the item column gives;
and the movement of a student between categories from start to end is a
comparison of two rows of the same student and item, which the quiz
column makes possible.
Empty accounts are left out: a box the student skipped is not an account
to code.
The verbatim answer is kept as the student typed it, not normalised, so
the coder reads what the student wrote.

<<write the coding sheet>>=
def open_records(rows, quiz, stems):
    """Return one record per student and open item, with the stem."""
    records = []
    for row in rows:
        for item in OPEN_ITEMS:
            column = column_for(item, row.keys())
            answer = row.get(column, "").strip() if column else ""
            if answer:
                records.append({"quiz": quiz, STUDENT: row[STUDENT],
                                LOGIN_COLUMN: row.get(LOGIN_COLUMN, ""),
                                COURSE_COLUMN: row.get(COURSE_COLUMN, ""),
                                "item": item, "stem": stems.get(item, item),
                                "answer": answer})
    return records


def write_coding_sheet(records, path, suggestions=None):
    """Write the accounts with coding columns to path."""
    fields = (["quiz", STUDENT, LOGIN_COLUMN, COURSE_COLUMN, "item", "answer"]
              + (SUGGESTED_COLUMNS if suggestions else []) + CODING_COLUMNS)
    with open(path, "w", newline="") as out:
        writer = csv.DictWriter(out, fieldnames=fields, extrasaction="ignore")
        writer.writeheader()
        for index, record in enumerate(records):
            if suggestions:
                record = {**record, **suggestions[index]}
            writer.writerow(record)
@

\subsubsection{Putting it together}

The main function reads or fetches one or both reports, filters by
consent, and reports each quiz: the opening item, the knowledge items
one by one, the scores.
With both quizzes it adds the paired gains, and it always writes the
coding sheet for whatever accounts it has read.
The quiz file defaults to the tangled end quiz next to the program,
since that is the one that carries every item.

<<analysis main>>=
def read_rows(path):
    """Read a saved student-analysis report."""
    with open(path, newline="") as infile:
        return list(csv.DictReader(infile))


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--quiz", choices=["start", "end", "both"],
                        default="start", help="which quiz to analyse")
    parser.add_argument("--results", nargs="+", metavar="CSV",
                        help="read these CSVs (start, end) instead of "
                             "fetching from Canvas")
    parser.add_argument("--quiz-file",
                        default=os.path.join(
                            os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)),
                            QUIZ_FILE),
                        help="quiz description to read the answer key from")
    parser.add_argument("--llm", action="store_true",
                        help="pre-code the open accounts with an LLM")
    parser.add_argument("--coding-sheet", default="quiz-coding-sheet.csv",
                        help="output CSV for the coding of the accounts")
    args = parser.parse_args()

    which = ["start", "end"] if args.quiz == "both" else [args.quiz]
    if args.results:
        if len(args.results) != len(which):
            sys.exit(f"expected {len(which)} results file(s) for {args.quiz}")
        rows = dict(zip(which, map(read_rows, args.results)))
    else:
        rows = {quiz: fetch_results(quiz) for quiz in which}
    if not all(rows.values()):
        sys.exit("no responses")
    knowledge, stems = load_quiz(args.quiz_file)

    students = consenting(rows["start"]) if "start" in rows else None
    for quiz in which:
        rows[quiz] = filter_consent(rows[quiz], students)
        print(f"\n== {QUIZ_TITLES[quiz]}: {len(rows[quiz])} students")
        for item in CONTROL_ITEMS:
            tabulate(rows[quiz], item)
        item_table(rows[quiz], knowledge)
        summarize_scores(rows[quiz])
    if args.quiz == "both":
        paired_gain(rows["start"], rows["end"], knowledge)

    records = [record for quiz in which
               for record in open_records(rows[quiz], quiz, stems)]
    suggestions = llm_precode(records) if args.llm else None
    write_coding_sheet(records, args.coding_sheet, suggestions)
    print(f"\ncoding sheet written to {args.coding_sheet}"
          f" ({len(records)} accounts)")
@
